Inheritance lets you build a new class on top of an existing one, reusing its code and extending it.

```java
class Animal {
    String name;
    void eat() { System.out.println(name + " eats"); }
    void breathe() { System.out.println("Breathing"); }
}

class Dog extends Animal {  // Dog IS-A Animal
    void bark() { System.out.println("Woof!"); }
}

Dog d = new Dog();
d.name = "Rex";   // inherited from Animal
d.eat();          // inherited method
d.breathe();      // inherited method
d.bark();         // Dog's own method
```

`Dog` inherits `name`, `eat()`, and `breathe()` from `Animal`. It doesn't need to rewrite them. `private` members of `Animal` are **not accessible** in `Dog` — only `public` and `protected` members are.

Java supports **single inheritance** for classes — a class can only `extend` one other class.

Every constructor in Java implicitly starts with `super()` if you don't write it:

```java
class Vehicle {
    String type;
    Vehicle(String type) {
        this.type = type;
        System.out.println("Vehicle: " + type);
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    String brand;
    Car(String brand) {
        super("Car"); // MUST be first — parent has no no-arg constructor
        this.brand = brand;
        System.out.println("Car: " + brand);
    }
}

new Car("Toyota");
// Output:
// Vehicle: Car
// Car: Toyota
```

If `Vehicle` only has a parameterized constructor and the `Car` constructor doesn't call `super("Car")`, the compiler gives an error: *implicit super() is undefined for Vehicle*.

The rule: `super()` must always be the **first statement** in a constructor. This guarantees parent initialization always completes before child initialization.

```java
class Parent {
    void instanceMethod() { System.out.println("Parent instance"); }
    static void staticMethod() { System.out.println("Parent static"); }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void instanceMethod() { System.out.println("Child instance"); } // OVERRIDE
    static void staticMethod() { System.out.println("Child static"); } // HIDE
}

Parent ref = new Child(); // reference type = Parent, object type = Child
ref.instanceMethod(); // Child instance — RUNTIME dispatch (overriding)
ref.staticMethod();  // Parent static — COMPILE-TIME dispatch (hiding)!
```

For instance methods, the JVM looks at the **actual object** (`new Child()`) at runtime — so `Child.instanceMethod()` runs. For static methods, the compiler uses the **reference type** (`Parent`) — so `Parent.staticMethod()` runs.

Static methods are not polymorphic. They belong to the class, not to an object.

```java
class Shape {
    String color = "white";
    void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing shape"); }
    Shape(String color) { this.color = color; }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    String color = "red"; // hides parent field

    Circle(String color) {
        super(color);      // 1. Call parent constructor
    }

    void draw() {
        super.draw();      // 2. Call parent method — extend, don't replace
        System.out.println("Drawing circle");
    }

    void showColors() {
        System.out.println(super.color); // 3. Access parent field
        System.out.println(this.color);  // access own field
    }
}
```

The most common and important use is calling `super.method()` inside an overriding method to **extend** the parent's behavior rather than completely replacing it. This is the standard pattern in frameworks like Spring and Android.

```java
// IS-A — Inheritance
class Animal { void breathe() {} }
class Dog extends Animal {  // Dog IS-A Animal
    void bark() {}
}

// HAS-A — Composition
class Engine {
    void start() { System.out.println("Engine started"); }
}
class Car {
    private Engine engine = new Engine(); // Car HAS-A Engine
    void drive() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Driving");
    }
}
```

**When IS-A (inheritance)?** When the subclass truly IS a specialized version of the parent. `Dog` is truly an `Animal`. The relationship is stable and unlikely to change.

**When HAS-A (composition)?** When you want to **reuse behavior** without a strict type relationship. A `Car` isn't an `Engine` — it *has* one. You can also swap the engine (dependency injection).

The principle **"Favor composition over inheritance"** (GoF) exists because composition is more flexible — you can change components at runtime, and there's no tight coupling between class hierarchies.
